{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Titan Revenue Forecast: Segment Drivers and Scenario Toggle",
  "scenario": "You're a junior analyst on the Mumbai desk covering Titan Company Limited. The PM wants a driver-based revenue model—not a single growth number, but a segmented forecast showing volume, price, and same-store-sales growth by business unit. Build Titan's FY25–FY27 revenue model with a scenario toggle (Base vs Optimistic) and prove your growth assumptions with a sensitivity table on price and store count.",
  "instructions": "Build a segmented revenue model for Titan's four divisions (Jewellery, Watches, Eyewear, Emerging Brands) using volume, price, store count, and same-store-sales-growth drivers. Create a scenario toggle in column C to switch between Base and Optimistic cases, compute total revenue by segment and year, and construct a two-way sensitivity table showing how Jewellery revenue changes with gold price realisation and store count. Do not use a PivotTable; write formulas that the PM can audit.",
  "learning_objective": "Build a multi-segment revenue model from driver-level assumptions (volume, SSSG, store count, price), construct a scenario toggle to test upside vs base case, and use a sensitivity table to isolate which drivers move the needle most on consolidated revenue.",
  "hints": [
    "Start by laying out the FY24 actuals and forecast assumptions (SSSG %, new store count, price per unit) in separate rows, then multiply them together: Revenue = Existing stores × Rev per store × (1 + SSSG) + New stores × Ramp factor. Use IF() to switch Scenario values.",
    "A scenario toggle is just an IF statement that reads a dropdown cell (e.g., 'Base' or 'Optimistic') and returns different assumption values. Example: =IF(Scenario=$B$1, 'optimistic_value', 'base_value'). Put the scenario choice in one cell (like C2), then reference it in all driver formulas.",
    "For the sensitivity table, create a two-row header (gold price on one axis, store count on the other), then use INDEX-MATCH or nested IFs to pull the revenue outcome for each price-store combination. Alternatively, use INDIRECT to reference the recalculated revenue cell with substituted inputs."
  ],
